From: gstreamer-custom-build local patches
Subject: [PATCH v2] h264decoder: recover from an unpairable field instead of wedging

gst_h264_decoder_find_first_field_picture() rejects the incoming slice when the
previously decoded field cannot be paired with it, which happens when

  a) a frame picture arrives while a first field is still pending,
  b) the pending first field belongs to a different frame_num, or
  c) the pending first field has the same parity as the incoming field.

In all three cases the pending field can never be completed, so it has to be
dropped. Upstream only does that for a field still held in priv->last_field.
When the orphaned field has already been placed in the DPB, the code merely
leaves a

    /* FIXME: implement fill gap field picture if it is already in DPB */

comment and does nothing, then returns FALSE so that
gst_h264_decoder_parse_slice() fails the slice with GST_FLOW_ERROR.

That combination is unrecoverable. find_first_field_picture() always looks at
the *last* entry of the DPB, so the orphan it just refused to pair is exactly
what the next slice finds as well. Every following slice therefore takes the
same error path and the decoder never outputs another frame; it only logs

    Couldn't find or determine first picture
    error: Failed to decode data

for each incoming slice until the pipeline is torn down. GstVideoDecoder's
"max-errors" property defaults to -1 (unlimited), so _gst_video_decoder_error()
never posts GST_MESSAGE_ERROR either and the application is not even told that
anything went wrong - the video just freezes with the last (partially decoded,
visibly corrupted) picture on screen.

This is trivially reachable with live interlaced (PAFF) broadcast streams over
RTP/UDP, where losing a single packet can drop one field of a pair. Observed on
an Intel N150 iGPU with vah264dec decoding a 1920x1080i H.264 stream received
over RTP/MPEG-2 TS multicast: after a single-packet loss the decoder emitted the
above error for every subsequent slice and never recovered.

Fix it by discarding the orphaned field in both cases - clearing
priv->last_field as before, or evicting the picture from the DPB by resetting
its output/reference flags and letting gst_h264_dpb_delete_unused() reap it -
and then returning TRUE with *first_field = NULL. The caller then simply starts
a new picture from the current slice, so decoding resumes with at most the
picture whose field was lost being damaged.

v2: also release the picture's GstVideoCodecFrame.

The videodecoder base class keeps every submitted frame on its internal list
until it is either finished or released, and for va* subclasses that frame's
output_buffer is what actually owns the decoded VA surface (exported as a
dmabuf). v1 only dropped the GstH264Picture, so each recovery leaked one
frame - and with it one surface/dmabuf fd. A one-hour soak against a stream
that hits the unpairable-field condition every ~11 minutes showed the process
fd count and GPU memory growing 1:1 with the recoveries (47 recoveries ->
+49 fds, +172 MiB GPU memory), while a stream without such events stayed
perfectly flat. Mirror what gst_h264_decoder_clear_dpb() does when discarding
without output: look the frame up by the picture's system_frame_number and
gst_video_decoder_release_frame() it before dropping the picture itself.

Note that an orphaned field may still be marked as a reference. Dropping a
reference can make subsequent pictures that refer to it decode with artefacts,
but a half-decoded field is unusable as a reference anyway, and the only
alternative is a decoder that is stuck forever.
--- a/_p2_/gst-libs/gst/codecs/gsth264decoder.c
+++ b/_p2_/gst-libs/gst/codecs/gsth264decoder.c
@@ -1165,6 +1165,91 @@
   return new_picture;
 }
 
+/* Local fix: discard an unpaired ("orphaned") field picture so that decoding
+ * can recover.
+ *
+ * gst_h264_decoder_find_first_field_picture() detects three situations in
+ * which the previously decoded field cannot be paired with the field that is
+ * about to be decoded:
+ *
+ *   a) a frame picture arrives while a first field is still pending,
+ *   b) the pending first field belongs to a different frame_num,
+ *   c) the pending first field has the same parity as the incoming field.
+ *
+ * All three mean the pending field can never be completed and must be thrown
+ * away. Upstream only handles the case where that field is still held in
+ * priv->last_field; when the field already sits in the DPB the original code
+ * merely leaves a "FIXME: implement fill gap field picture if it is already in
+ * DPB" comment and does nothing at all, so the orphan stays in the DPB
+ * forever. Because gst_h264_decoder_find_first_field_picture() always inspects
+ * the *last* DPB entry, every subsequent slice keeps finding the very same
+ * orphan, fails again, and the decoder never produces another frame: it just
+ * emits "Couldn't find or determine first picture" for every incoming slice
+ * until the pipeline is torn down.
+ *
+ * This is easy to hit in the field with live interlaced (PAFF) broadcast
+ * streams over RTP/UDP: a single lost packet can drop one field of a pair,
+ * and from that moment on the decoder is wedged. Since GstVideoDecoder's
+ * "max-errors" property defaults to -1 (tolerate an unlimited number of
+ * errors), no GST_MESSAGE_ERROR is ever posted either, so the application
+ * cannot even detect the condition - the video simply freezes.
+ *
+ * Evict the orphan from the DPB (by clearing its output/reference flags and
+ * letting gst_h264_dpb_delete_unused() reap it) so that the next picture can
+ * be decoded normally.
+ *
+ * NOTE: an orphaned field may still be marked as a reference picture. Dropping
+ * a reference can make later pictures that refer to it decode with artefacts,
+ * but such a half-decoded field is unusable as a reference anyway and the only
+ * alternative is a permanently stuck decoder, so discarding it is strictly
+ * better.
+ *
+ * @prev_field must not be dereferenced by the caller after this call: it may
+ * have been freed when it was removed from the DPB.
+ */
+static void
+gst_h264_decoder_drop_unpaired_field (GstH264Decoder * self,
+    GstH264Picture * prev_field, gboolean in_dpb)
+{
+  GstH264DecoderPrivate *priv = self->priv;
+  GstVideoCodecFrame *frame;
+
+  GST_DEBUG_OBJECT (self,
+      "Discarding unpaired field picture %p (frame_num %d, poc %d, field %d, "
+      "in_dpb %d) so that decoding can resume", prev_field,
+      prev_field->frame_num, prev_field->pic_order_cnt, prev_field->field,
+      in_dpb);
+
+  /* Also drop the GstVideoCodecFrame the videodecoder base class still
+   * tracks for this picture. This mirrors what gst_h264_decoder_clear_dpb()
+   * does when it empties the DPB without outputting: unreffing only the
+   * GstH264Picture is not enough, because the frame's output_buffer is what
+   * actually owns the decoder surface (for va* subclasses a VA surface
+   * exported as a dmabuf). While the frame stays on the base class's list
+   * that buffer can never return to the pool, so every recovery would
+   * otherwise leak one surface/dmabuf fd - observed as fd counts growing
+   * 1:1 with these recoveries during long-run testing. */
+  frame = gst_video_decoder_get_frame (GST_VIDEO_DECODER (self),
+      GST_CODEC_PICTURE_FRAME_NUMBER (prev_field));
+  if (frame)
+    gst_video_decoder_release_frame (GST_VIDEO_DECODER (self), frame);
+
+  if (!in_dpb) {
+    /* Not in the DPB yet, still owned by priv->last_field. */
+    gst_clear_h264_picture (&priv->last_field);
+    return;
+  }
+
+  /* gst_h264_dpb_delete_unused() only reaps pictures that are neither needed
+   * for output nor used for reference. */
+  prev_field->needed_for_output = FALSE;
+  prev_field->ref = GST_H264_PICTURE_REF_NONE;
+  prev_field->ref_pic = FALSE;
+
+  gst_h264_dpb_delete_unused (priv->dpb);
+  /* prev_field may be freed at this point. */
+}
+
 static gboolean
 gst_h264_decoder_find_first_field_picture (GstH264Decoder * self,
     GstH264Slice * slice, GstH264Picture ** first_field)
@@ -1235,13 +1320,22 @@
   return TRUE;
 
 error:
-  if (!in_dpb) {
-    gst_clear_h264_picture (&priv->last_field);
-  } else {
-    /* FIXME: implement fill gap field picture if it is already in DPB */
-  }
+  /* Local fix: the pending field can never be completed. Throw it away
+   * (including when it already lives in the DPB, which upstream does not
+   * handle - see gst_h264_decoder_drop_unpaired_field() for the full
+   * rationale) and report success with no first field, so that the caller
+   * starts a brand new picture from the current slice instead of failing.
+   *
+   * Returning FALSE here (the upstream behaviour) makes
+   * gst_h264_decoder_parse_slice() return GST_FLOW_ERROR for this slice, and
+   * because the orphan used to be left in the DPB the very next slice hit the
+   * exact same failure, wedging the decoder permanently. Recovering in place
+   * keeps the decoder running and costs at most the picture whose field was
+   * lost. */
+  gst_h264_decoder_drop_unpaired_field (self, prev_field, in_dpb);
 
-  return FALSE;
+  *first_field = NULL;
+  return TRUE;
 }
 
 static GstFlowReturn
